CM ============================================================
CM  noise_canceller_array.nec — TM-RFI-NEC-003 Rev A
CM  Two-Antenna Noise Canceller Array: Main + Reference Antenna
CM  Frequency: 7.15 MHz (40m)
CM
CM  Geometry:
CM    Main antenna: full-size λ/4 vertical at origin
CM    Reference antenna: short λ/20 vertical, 2m away (simulates nearby noise whip)
CM    Ground: average real ground
CM
CM  Analysis:
CM    Model 1: Main antenna alone (reference to compare)
CM    Model 2: Reference antenna pattern (what it picks up vs. main)
CM    Model 3: Combined pattern with phase cancellation
CM             (simulates optimal noise cancellation)
CM
CM  Key question: How much desired signal is lost when cancelling noise?
CM  If noise source is at 0° azimuth and desired signal at 90°:
CM    - Can noise be cancelled while preserving signal?
CM    - What is the "signal preserved to noise cancelled" ratio?
CM ============================================================
CM ── MAIN ANTENNA: λ/4 vertical at origin ─────────────────────
CM Height: 10.15m (λ/4 at 7.15 MHz)
CM Tag 1: main vertical
CM ── REFERENCE ANTENNA: short vertical 2m north ───────────────
CM Height: 2.1m (λ/20 at 7.15 MHz — typical short noise whip)
CM Tag 2: reference antenna
CM ── RADIAL GROUND PLANE (4 × λ/4 radials for main antenna) ───
CM ── REAL GROUND ───────────────────────────────────────────────
CM ── FREQUENCY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
CM ── EXCITATION ─────────────────────────────────���───────────────
CM MODEL 1: Main antenna only (excite tag 1)
CM MODEL 2: Reference antenna only (uncomment, comment out above):
CM EX 0 2 1 0 1.0 0.0
CM MODEL 3: Both with cancellation phasing (uncomment, comment out above):
CM Main fed normally: EX 0 1 1 0 1.0 0.0
CM Reference fed 180° out of phase with scaling factor 0.5 (amplitude match to noise):
CM EX 0 2 1 0 -0.5 0.0
CM (In practice, phase and amplitude set by noise canceller hardware)
CM ── RADIATION PATTERNS ─────────────────────────────────────────
CM Azimuth: noise often arrives from specific bearing; check full 360°
CM Elevation: low-angle DX arrives at 5–20°; noise often at high angles
CM ============================================================
CM  EXPECTED RESULTS:
CM
CM  MODEL 1 (Main vertical only):
CM    Pattern: omnidirectional in azimuth (perfect vertical)
CM    Gain: ~0 dBi at 10° elevation (λ/4 vertical with 4 radials)
CM    DX performance: good low-angle radiation
CM
CM  MODEL 2 (Short reference whip only):
CM    Pattern: omnidirectional in azimuth (small vertical)
CM    Gain: very low (~−10 dBi) — short whip, high radiation resistance mismatch
CM    Angular response: nearly same shape as main antenna
CM    Key: reference antenna picks up SAME NOISE from most directions as main antenna
CM    This is WHY noise cancellation works: similar noise pattern in both antennas
CM
CM  MODEL 3 (Combined with cancellation):
CM    The combination V_main - k × V_ref creates a cardioid pattern:
CM    G(θ) = G_main(θ) - k × G_ref(θ)
CM    For k = amplitude ratio that cancels a specific direction:
CM    If noise comes from 0° azimuth (North), set k so G_main(0°) = k × G_ref(0°)
CM    Then at 0°: V_main(noise) - k × V_ref(noise) ≈ 0 (null)
CM    At 90°: desired signal NOT cancelled (reference sees same signal as main)
CM    → Signal partially preserved while noise from 0° is cancelled
CM
CM  PRACTICAL LIMITATIONS:
CM    1. Phase difference between main and reference must be compensated (choke/cable)
CM    2. Nearby noise source (1m–10m): near-field geometry; patterns differ from far-field
CM    3. Multiple noise sources: can only null ONE at a time (single auxiliary antenna)
CM    4. For multiple nulls: need multiple auxiliary antennas (Howells-type array)
CM
CM  SIGNAL PRESERVATION:
CM    With null at 0° (noise), signal from 90° preserved at ~−3 dB of original
CM    (one lobe of cardioid has 3 dB less gain than omnidirectional maximum)
CM    Net gain: +20 dB improvement in S/N if noise was 20 dB above signal
CM    Net gain less 3 dB signal: +17 dB net S/N improvement. Excellent result.
CM ============================================================
